<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Rostock</h1><p class="st-meta">1229 CE – 1314 CE (approx.) · capital Rostock</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Short-lived Mecklenburg lordship of the Niklotid princes around the Hanseatic port of Rostock, which fell under Danish and then Mecklenburg rule.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Prince Henry</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-borwin-ii-lord-of-mecklenburg">Borwin II</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-mecklenburg">Mecklenburg</a> died in 1226, his lands were divided among his sons in 1229. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-borwin-iii-lord-of-rostock">Henry Borwin III</a> received the share around Rostock, a town that had received <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-lubeck">Lübeck</a> law in 1218. He and his heirs ruled as lords of Rostock, one of four Mecklenburg branches alongside Mecklenburg, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-werle">Werle</a> and Parchim-Richenberg.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The lords were descendants of the Obotrite prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niklot">Niklot</a>, ruling a land newly settled by German farmers and townspeople. Rostock itself grew fast as a Hanseatic port and gained wide self-government, often at odds with its lords. The territory was small and the lords were pressed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and their <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-mecklenburg">Mecklenburg</a> cousins, and in 1300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-the-child">Nicholas the Child</a> placed himself under Danish protection.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-the-child">Nicholas the Child</a> died without a son in 1314, the lordship passed under Danish control and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-lord-of-mecklenburg">Henry II of Mecklenburg</a>, who secured it by 1323. Rostock remained part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-mecklenburg">Mecklenburg</a>, while the city kept its Hanseatic autonomy.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lordship-of-rostock.jpg" alt="Lordship of Rostock" width="320" height="252"><figcaption>Image: Alphathon /ˈæɫfə.θɒn · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lordship_of_Rostock_locator_map_(1250).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1229 CE – 1314 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rostock</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Henry Borwin III, Waldemar of Rostock, Nicholas the Child</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rostock</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1272&amp;focus=lordship-of-rostock" aria-label="Lordship of Rostock on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4948 322 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Rostock lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5268 578L5254 584M5268 578L5274 535"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5254 584h0M5274 535h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5268 578h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5268 578h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Rostock. <a href="/atlas?year=1272&amp;focus=lordship-of-rostock">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
